Description: Quick: draw a framed window as a pair where its platform draws the frame
 A window whose style shows the depth of its elements asks for two views
 of its surface and, once the surface has them, is drawn once per eye. A
 window with a frame drawn by the client around it (client-side
 decorations: a compositor without server-side decorations, or a program
 that asks for its own frame) was always drawn once, since the frame is no
 part of the pair; but it asked for the two views all the same, got them,
 and showed the window's one picture in both eyes while the platform
 marked it as the desktop's own depth.
 .
 A platform that draws such a frame into both eyes, the same picture at
 the screen's plane, says so with the window's
 _q_stereo_csd_pair_supported, as the Wayland EGL platform does; such a
 window is now drawn as a pair. Where the platform does not say so it is
 drawn once, as before.
 .
 The pair now also ends before the request for views changes with the
 depth. The Wayland EGL platform reads the window's _q_stereo_pair when
 that request changes, as the mark of an engine that composes its pair
 into one picture (Qt Widgets, whose framed pair goes side by side into
 one buffer). A framed window turned flat and back quickly could carry
 the pair of its last frame into a new request, and the platform then
 declared the surface a second time while the EGL implementation had
 declared it: a protocol error that ended the program.
 .
 tst_qquickwindowstereo::drawsAFramedWindowAsAPair() shows a window
 inside a frame the client draws and expects its pair, and the pair's end
 at once when its depth changes; it fails on +stereo3d3 and on the first
 form of this change.
